ly.  \n\nBut passing carries the risk that the peasants start playing combinations I cannot easily beat. However, given that the last play is a low single and both opponents passed earlier in the game, they may not have strong follow-ups immediately.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nI choose to **pass** because:  \n- ♣10 is low; saving my high singles (♠2, ♣A) for stronger opponent plays later is better.  \n- I want to see what Next Player does after Prev Player’s ♣10 — if Next Player plays a medium single (e.g., J, Q, K), I can beat it with ♦Q/♦K/♣A without using my 2s.  \n- Passing conserves my power and keeps options open for bombs or sequences later, especially since I have multiple 2s and potential to control the endgame.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"cards": []}'}
